The expected behavior when the element to search for is not present in the array is that the function should return a tuple indicating that the element was not found. A common approach is to return `(False, -1)` where `False` indicates the element was not found and `-1` (or another sentinel value) represents an invalid index. This clearly communicates the absence of the search element in the array.

Here's how you can modify the function to handle this case:

```python
def sequential_search(arr, target):
    for i in range(len(arr)):
        if arr[i] == target:
            return (True, i)
    return (False, -1)

# Test cases
assert sequential_search([11,23,58,31,56,77,43,12,65,19], 31) == (True, 3)
assert sequential_search([12, 32, 45, 62, 35, 47, 44, 61], 61) == (True, 7)
assert sequential_search([9, 10, 17, 19, 22, 39, 48, 56], 48) == (True, 6)
assert sequential_search([11,23,58,31,56,77,43,12,65,19], 60) == (False, -1)
```

This implementation ensures that the function behaves consistently whether the element is found or not.
